  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 026: High-NA EUV can print finer features, but chipmakers must weigh that improvement against changes in manufacturing cost and complexity.

Lesson 027: A pellicle protects the chip's master pattern from particles, helping prevent the same defect from being printed repeatedly.

Lesson 028: An EUV mask uses carefully built mirrors and patterned materials to direct light into the shapes needed on a wafer.

Lesson 029: Mask inspection and repair catch important defects before a faulty master pattern can spoil many chips.

Lesson 030: Mask writers make the master patterns used to print chips, so slow mask production can delay a new design.

The big idea

You now understand much of what happens inside a wafer fab. This lesson begins a new unit: following the finished silicon until it becomes useful AI.

The question is simple: if a fab produces 20% more working accelerator dies, can customers do 20% more AI work?

Only if the rest of the system can use them. Learning to find the limiting step is the reason we studied the manufacturing details.

1. Put the whole system back on the map

Chip design → wafer fabrication → tested dies → packaging with memory → servers and networking → powered, cooled clusters → software running models → useful AI output

This is a dependency map, not one factory conveyor belt. Memory chips are made separately. Data centers and power infrastructure can be built while accelerator dies are still in production. Software influences the chip design before manufacturing even starts.

The branches must meet at the right time. A finished logic die cannot replace a missing memory stack, and a delivered server cannot replace an unfinished electrical connection.

TSMC's CoWoS documentation illustrates one of those meeting points: packaging integrates compute silicon and high-bandwidth memory into a product. The following lessons explain how.

2. Three different questions hide inside the word bottleneck

Manufacturing: how many working accelerators can suppliers deliver per month? Wafer output, memory supply, assembly and test all matter.

Deployment: how many delivered accelerators can the customer put into service? Server integration, networking, power, cooling and commissioning matter here.

Runtime: how much useful work can the installed system complete? The workload and software determine how effectively it uses compute, memory and communication.

These require different evidence. Scanner uptime helps answer a manufacturing question. It cannot, by itself, explain why an installed GPU is waiting for data.

3. A small model makes the distinction concrete

The following numbers are invented teaching assumptions, not estimates of any company. Consider one product needing one good logic die and six qualified HBM stacks per accelerator.

Assume a steady monthly flow, no starting inventory, and capacities already adjusted for losses and allocated to this product:

- Logic supply: enough for 100,000 accelerators.

- HBM supply: 480,000 stacks, enough for 80,000 accelerators.

- Packaging and test: 90,000 working accelerators.

- Site deployment: 70,000 additional accelerators brought into service.

Manufacturing is capped at 80,000 accelerators per month by HBM. Deployment is capped at 70,000 per month by site readiness. Output above deployment capacity accumulates as inventory in this simplified example.

Now raise good logic output from 100,000 to 120,000. Neither limit changes. More finished silicon alone creates no additional operational accelerators that month.

This does not make wafer capacity unimportant. It means its marginal value depends on what is available beside and downstream of it.

